In a club of size \(s\), there are \(\binom{s}{3}\) equally likely groups of three. To include both co-presidents, the only choice is the third member, giving \(s-2\) favorable groups. For the clubs of sizes 5, 7, and 8, the respective probabilities are \(3/10,\ 5/35=1/7,\ 6/56=3/28\).
Since the clubs are equally likely, average these probabilities: \(\frac13(\frac3{10}+\frac17+\frac3{28})=\frac{42+20+15}{420}=\frac{11}{60}\).
Final answer: \dfrac{11}{60}
